        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat(google-meet): retain the full caption transcript per session (#103387)",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nImplements #103387.\n\nThe Chrome transport scrapes Meet captions into an in-page buffer capped at 50 lines, and `status` exposed only the last 5 as `recentTranscript` — so for any real meeting the transcript was mostly discarded, and observe-mode users had to build fragile external pollers that still lose lines during fast speech. This follows the accumulation design proposed in the issue.\n\n### Change\n\n- `extensions/google-meet/src/transports/chrome.ts`: the status script now also ships its full in-page buffer as `transcriptWindow` (≤50 lines); `recentTranscript` stays at 5 lines for compatibility.\n- `extensions/google-meet/src/runtime.ts`: the gateway merges `transcriptWindow` into a durable per-session transcript on every health refresh (join + status/health polls), deduped by the page-recorded `at`/`speaker`/`text` key, capped at the most recent **2000 lines** (fixed constant — no new config surface per the repo's config bar; `droppedLines` reports trimming). The window is merge input only and is stripped before health lands in status payloads, so `status` responses stay the same size. New `transcript(sessionId, { sinceIndex? })` refreshes browser health for active sessions and returns lines with absolute indices: `nextIndex` from one call is the `sinceIndex` for the next, which fixes the issue's \"poll gap\" problem exactly.\n- `extensions/google-meet/index.ts`: new `googlemeet.transcript` gateway method and `transcript` tool action with optional `sinceIndex`.\n- `extensions/google-meet/src/cli.ts`: `openclaw googlemeet transcript <session-id> [--since <index>] [--json]`.\n- `docs/plugins/google-meet.md`: observe-only section + tool action table updated.\n\nNot included (flagged in the issue as optional): `chrome.transcriptFile` persistence — repo storage policy points that at SQLite rather than a JSONL sidecar, which is a separate product decision.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `pnpm test extensions/google-meet` — 17 files, 207 tests passed. New regressions: overlapping page windows across polls accumulate without duplicates and the `sinceIndex` cursor returns exactly zero new lines at `nextIndex`; the cap test proves 2100 merged lines yield `droppedLines: 100`, retained lines 100..2099 in order (this test caught and fixed a dedupe-eviction bug during development); status payload health carries no `transcriptWindow`.\n- `pnpm tsgo:extensions`, `pnpm tsgo:extensions:test`, oxfmt, oxlint — clean.\n\n## Real behavior proof\n\nBehavior addressed: caption lines beyond a 5-line window over a 50-line page buffer were irrecoverable; no way to retrieve a meeting transcript after the fact.\nReal environment tested: the plugin's real join/status/transcript gateway methods driven through the existing stubbed browser-proxy harness, with the page status script's real JSON contract (including the new `transcriptWindow`) exercised end to end.\nExact steps or command run after this patch: `pnpm test extensions/google-meet`\nEvidence after fix: joining with page windows `[1..3]` then polling with `[2..5]` yields transcript lines 1..5 exactly once with `nextIndex: 5`; `sinceIndex: 5` returns no lines; a 2100-line window yields `droppedLines: 100` with lines 100..2099 retained in order.\nObserved result after fix: 207/207 tests pass; the transcript survives beyond the page window and is retrievable incrementally via CLI, gateway method, and tool action.\nWhat was not tested: a live Google Meet call with real captions; the merge consumes the same parsed status payload shape the live path produces, and the page-side change is a one-line widening of what the existing scraper already records.\n",
        "branch": "feat/103387-googlemeet-transcript-retention",
